\description{
A widget that renders the bio.viz association scatter: one point per
participant, with a variable on each axis, a correlation coefficient printed
under each panel and, when asked for, a fitted line over the points. The
coefficient and the line are computed here, in R, by \code{\link[=Analyze_Correlation]{Analyze_Correlation()}}
and \code{\link[=Analyze_Fit]{Analyze_Fit()}}, and shipped with the page, so a saved page shows them
with no R and no network.
}
\section{What the page opens on}{

The pair the settings name in \code{x} and \code{y}, each a variable as bio.viz writes
one: \code{list(measure = "IL-6", visit = "Week 4")} for a biomarker's result at a
visit, with \code{value = "change"}, \code{"fold_change"}, \code{"percent_change"} or
\code{"baseline"} for a value worked out from its baseline, or
\code{list(col = "AGE")} for a participant-level number. With neither named the
chart opens on the first two biomarkers at the first visit.
}

\section{Statistics shipped with the page}{

The chart computes no coefficient and no line. It asks R for a coefficient
per panel and, for a linear fit or a smooth, a line per panel, and in a
widget the answers are worked out when the widget is made and stored in the
page, on the rows the chart draws in each panel of the view the settings
open on: its two variables, colour, panel column, filters and scales.

For that view the widget stores both coefficients, Pearson's and
Spearman's, and both lines, the linear fit and the smooth, whichever the
settings open on: four results for each panel. The Method control and the
Fitted line control change what is asked of the same rows and nothing else,
so a reader of a saved page can move them and still be answered. The line
y = x is the chart's own and needs no R.

A reader who moves any other control, to another variable, value type,
visit, colour, panel column, filter or scale, is told that statistics are
unavailable for that view, and a fitted line is not drawn. The page never
shows one view's numbers under another.

On a logarithmic axis R is given the base-10 logarithm of the value, as the
chart gives it, so a coefficient or a line there is of the values as
plotted. The scale is part of what a stored result is found by.

The page states which R computed the results: the R version, the gsm.bio
version and the time (see \link{StatisticsResult}). So that R and the chart
resolve the same rows, the widget names the baseline visits to the chart
outright, as \code{\link[=Widget_GroupComparison]{Widget_GroupComparison()}} does.
}

\section{Filters}{

With a participant table the chart has filters, set by the setting
\code{filters} under safety.viz's rules: a filter opens on its \code{start} when the
data has it and otherwise on All, a filter set \code{all = FALSE} has no All and
opens on its first value, and \code{multiple = TRUE} lets several values through.
R works out what each filter opens on as the chart does, and stores the
results for those participants.

The first value of an \code{all = FALSE} filter is the one exception, because
the chart lists a filter's values in the order of the reader's browser,
which R cannot know: for a letter with an accent or for punctuation it can
differ from R's order, by code point. So the widget hands the chart R's
first value as the filter's \code{start}, and the page opens on the participants
R computed for, though that value may not be the first in the list.
}

\section{Bundles}{

The widget loads bio.viz's bundle and the copy of safety.viz's bundle that
bio.viz itself builds its chart from. Both are copied from bio.viz, with the
bio.viz commit and a checksum per file recorded beside them in
\code{system.file("htmlwidgets", "lib", "SOURCE.json", package = "gsm.bio")}. They
are bio.viz v0.2.0 and safety.viz v1.9.0, the first safety.viz with the kit
the chart is built from, as bio.viz takes it from safety.viz's \code{dev} branch;
the record says from which commit. gsm.safety carries an earlier safety.viz
without the kit, and once it carries v1.9.0 the widgets can take the bundle
from there.
}

